  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure will continue to drift east of the area over the\nnext couple of days, resulting in increasing moisture and\ngradually increasing temperatures through Tuesday. Similar to\nyesterday, there will likely be virga through this evening with\nrain chances increasing slightly as shortwave energy aloft moves\ninto the region tonight through Monday. The best chance of\nshowers will be across western zones where there will be better\nlow to mid-level moisture. Upper level ridging will briefly \nbuild into the area late Monday night/early Tuesday which will \nnudge any showers out of the local area by Tuesday morning. Warm\nair advection will provide above normal temperatures through at\nleast Tuesday with highs in the mid to upper 60s Monday giving \nway to high temps in the low to mid 70s Tuesday afternoon. A few\nspots east of I-71 will likely reach the upper 70s on Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe pattern will start to shift late Tuesday into Wednesday as a\ndeep upper trough approaches from the west. A cold front \nwill approach the local area Wednesday and cross the CWA on \nThursday. This will result in a high probability of showers \nthroughout the day Wednesday with rain chances continuing into \nThursday. As of now, it appears that impacts from the mid to \nlate week system will be minimal. There may be some moderate to \nheavy rain, but recent dry weather will mitigate any flooding \nissues. There's also some potential for thunder on Wednesday, \nalthough instability will likely be marginal and severe weather\nis not anticipated. It appears that lake-enhanced rain chances \nwill linger through Friday and possibly into next weekend as an\nunsettled pattern aloft pushes a series of upper troughs across\nthe local area. Temperatures will remain above normal through \nthe middle of the week before moderating late week and into the \nweekend. By Saturday, highs are forecast to be in the cooler but\nmore seasonable 50s.",
